This is Erdős Problem 695, which is explicitly marked as an open research problem (the Lean declaration carries `@[category research open]`). The conjecture asks whether for any sequence of primes $q_1 < q_2 < \cdots$ satisfying $q_{i+1} \equiv 1 \pmod{q_i}$, one must have $\lim_{k\to\infty} q_k^{1/k} = \infty$, and the variant asks whether there exists such a sequence with $q(k) \le \exp(k(\log k)^{1+o(1)})$.

These are genuinely open problems in number theory. The constraint $q_{i+1} \equiv 1 \pmod{q_i}$ forces $q_i \mid (q_{i+1}-1)$, so $q_{i+1} \ge q_i + 1$ and in fact $q_{i+1} \ge 2q_i - 1$ would not even be guaranteed (only $q_{i+1} = 1 + m q_i$ for some $m \ge 1$, and primality of $q_{i+1}$). A trivial lower bound is $q_k \ge q_1 \cdot 2^{k-1}$-ish growth is not even forced since $m=1$ gives $q_{i+1} = q_i+1$ which is rarely prime. The question is whether the growth must be super-exponential ($q_k^{1/k}\to\infty$) or whether one can keep it near $\exp(k(\log k)^{1+o(1)})$.

This is a well-known open problem with no known resolution. I cannot produce a proof of an open research conjecture.

